/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:35:As $R'$ is consistent with $\sigma'$, $(v_{\ell},v_i,v_j)\in\mathcal{B}$. Together with the assumption that $(v_i,x,v_j)\in\mathcal{B}$, this implies that $(v_{\ell},x,v_j)\in\mathcal{B}$ (otherwise $R[\{v_{\ell},v_i,v_j,x\}]$ would be a conflict of size $4$). The proof that $(v_i,x,v_{\ell'})\in \mathcal{B}$  is symmetric. Knowing that $(v_i,x,v_{\ell'})\in \mathcal{B}$, as $\ell<i$ we can apply the above arguments on $\ell$ and $i,\ell'$ to prove that that $(v_{\ell},x,v_{\ell'})\in\mathcal{B}$.
