/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:240:%%Since $t$ and $abc$ are consistent with $\sigma$, it follows that $\{a,b,c,d\}$ is a conflict for any choice of $\{b,c,d\}$. This concludes the proof.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:249:%\begin{lemma}[Sunflower Lemma] \label{lem:flower}
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:258:%Observe that there exist two ways to edit the center $t$. Adapting techniques from~\cite{GM10}, we can fix this issue by setting $m > 2k$, and thus obtain a quadratic vertex-kernel for \BIT{}. Nevertheless, this is not enough to obtain a \emph{linear} vertex kernel. To that aim, we combine Conflict Packing and a sunflower rule on particular conflicts.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:263:%	Let $R_\sigma = (V, \mathcal{B}, \sigma)$ be an ordered instance of \BIT{}. A sunflower $\mathcal{S} = \{C_1, \dots, C_m\}$ of $R_\sigma$ is \emph{simple} if its center $t$ is the only triplet inconsistent in $R_\sigma[C_i]$, $1 \leqslant i \leqslant m$. 
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:277:%\paragraph{Conflict Packing.} Using Conflict Packing and the Simple sunflower rule, we prove that the \BIT{} problem can be solved in polynomial time on instances whose parameter satisfies $k < (|V|/5)$. The existence of this algorithm directly implies the existence of a linear vertex-kernel for \BIT{}, as stated in Corollary~\ref{coro:bit}. The notion of Conflict Packing for the \BIT{} problem is similar to the one for \RTI{} (Section~\ref{sec:safe}). Indeed, here again, two conflicts may share a triplet $t$ but still require two distinct editions. Hence, the notion of \emph{seed} is useful in this case too. 
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:293:%The proof of the following result is the same than the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:cprti}. 
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:303:%Let $R = (V, \mathcal{B})$ be an instance of \BIT{} and $\mathcal{C}$ be a conflict packing of $R$. There exists an ordering $\sigma$ such that every triplet $t$ inconsistent with $\sigma$ satisfies $V(t) \subseteq V(\mathcal{C})$. 
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:307:%	Let $V' = V \setminus V(\mathcal{C})$. Observe that $R' = R[V']$ is consistent with a (unique) ordering $\sigma'$. Moreover, notice that for every vertex $a \in V(\mathcal{C})$ the instance $R_a = R[V' \cup \{a\}]$ is consistent (otherwise $\mathcal{C}$ would not be maximal). Thereby there exists a unique ordering $\sigma'_a$ such that every triplet of $R_a$ is consistent with $\sigma'_a$. In other words, $\sigma'$ contains a unique pair of consecutive vertices $(u,w)$ such that we have $u <_{\sigma'_a} a <_{\sigma'_a} w$. We call such a pair the \emph{locus} of $a$.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:308:%	Using again the maximality argument on $\mathcal{C}$, we know that for any pair of vertices $a,b \in V(\mathcal{C})$ the instance $R_{ab} = R[V' \cup \{a,b\}]$ is consistent. Hence if $a$ and $b$ have different loci then $R_{ab}$ is clearly consistent with the ordering obtained from $\sigma'$ by inserting $a$ and $b$ in their respective loci. It remains to consider the case where $a$ and $b$ have the same locus. 
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:322:%	We now describe how the ordering $\sigma$ is built from $\sigma'$. For any two consecutive vertices $u$ and $w$ such that $L_{uw} \neq \emptyset$ we introduce the vertices of $L_{uw}$ according to the (strict) total order $<_{uw}$. We now prove that $\sigma$ satisfies the desired property. Let $t = \{a,b,c\}$ be any triplet of $\mathcal{B}$ such that $V(t) \cap V' \neq \emptyset$. Observe that if $t$ is such that $V(t) \subseteq V'$ then $t$ is consistent by construction. Next, if $|V(t) \cap V'| = 2$ then $t$ is again consistent by construction. Finally, if $|V(t) \cap V'| = 1$ with $a,b \in V(\mathcal{C})$, then two cases may occur: if $a$ and $b$ have different loci then $t$ is consistent since $R_{ab}$ is consistent. In the other case, we know that $t$ is consistent since $a$ and $b$ have been inserted in their common locus according to the strict total order $<_{uw}$. It follows that $\sigma$ is the sought ordering.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:329:%	Let $R = (V, \mathcal{B})$ be an instance of \BIT{} such that $k < (|V|/5)$. There exists an algorithm that either computes an edition of size at most $k$ or answers \textsc{No} in polynomial time. 
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:334:%	Let $ R = (V, \mathcal{B})$ be an instance of \BIT{}, and $\sigma$ be the ordering obtained through Lemma~\ref{lem:realcpbit}. We compute in polynomial time (for instance greedily) a conflict packing $\mathcal{C}$ of $R$. Observe that $|V| > 5k$ (since we assume $k < (|V|/5)$) and let $V' = V \setminus V(\mathcal{C})$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:cpbit}, we know that $|V(\mathcal{C})| \leqslant 4k$. Hence $V'$ contains at least $k + 1$ vertices that do not belong to any triplet inconsistent with $\sigma$ by construction.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:338:%		There exists a simple sunflower $\mathcal{S} = \{C_1, \dots, C_m\}$, $m > k$ that can be computed in polynomial time.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:342:%		Let $t$ be any triplet inconsistent with $\sigma$ and $V'' \subseteq V'$ be a set of $k + 1$ vertices of $V'$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:conflictbit} we know that $C_d = V(t) \cup \{d\}$ is a conflict for any vertex $d \in V''$. Assuming $\{d_1, \dots, d_{k+1}\} \subseteq V''$, it follows that $C_i = V(t) \cup \{d_i\}$, $1 \leqslant i \leqslant k+1$, is a conflict whose only inconsistent triplet is $t$. Hence $\mathcal{S} = \{C_1, \dots, C_{k+1}\}$ is a simple sunflower of center $t$.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:345:%	Using Rule~\ref{rule:simplesun} we have to edit the center $t \in \mathcal{B}$ of $\mathcal{S}$ w.r.t. to $\sigma$. Since $\sigma$ still contains at least $k + 1$ vertices that do not belong to any inconsistent triplet after this operation, every inconsistent triplet of $\sigma$ must be edited. Hence if $\sigma$ contains less than $k$ inconsistent triplets then editing such triplets is an edition of $R$ and we return answer \textsc{Yes}; otherwise we answer \textsc{No}.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:351:%	The \BIT{} problem admits a kernel with at most $5k$ vertices.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section4-betweenness.tex:355:%	Let $R = (V, \mathcal{B})$ be an instance of \BIT{}. By Theorem~\ref{thm:bit}, the problem can be solved in polynomial time if $k < (|V|/5)$. Hence we can assume $k \geqslant (|V|/5)$, which implies that $|V| \leqslant 5k$. 
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section2-tournaments.tex:3:In this section, we consider asymmetric and loopless directed graphs (digraphs for short). Let $D=(V,A)$ be a digraph. An arc oriented from vertex $u\in V$ to $v\in V$ is denoted $uv$. The \emph{in-neighbourhood} of a vertex $x$ is the set $N^-(x)=\{y\in V\mid yx\in A\}$ and the \emph{out-neighbourhood} of $x$ is $N^+(x)=\{y\in V\mid xy\in A\}$. If $V'\subseteq V$, then $D[V']=(V',A')$ is the subdigraph \emph{induced} by $V'$, that is $A'=\{uv \in A\mid u\in V', v\in V'\}$. Similarly, if $A'\subseteq A$, then $D[A']=(V',A')$ denotes the \emph{digraph} where $V'=\{v\in V\mid \exists uv\in A' \mbox{ or } vw\in A'\}$. We say that $D$ is \emph{acyclic} if it does not contain any circuit and that $D$ is \emph{transitive} if for every triple of vertices $\{u, v, w\}\subseteq V$ such that $uv\in A$ and $vw\in A$, then $uw\in A$. A \emph{tournament} $T=(V,A)$ is a complete asymmetric digraph and a \emph{bipartite tournament} $T=(X\uplus Y,A)$ is a complete bipartite digraph (there is an arc between $u$ and $v$ if and only if $u\in X$ and $v\in Y$). We denote by $\mathbf{FAS}(D)$ the size of a minimum feedback arc set of $D$.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section2-tournaments.tex:5:A vertex ordering $\sigma$ of $D$ is a total order on the vertex set $V$. We denote by $u<_{\sigma} v$ the fact that $u$ is smaller than $v$ in $\sigma$. It is well-known that every acyclic digraph has a \emph{topological ordering} $\sigma$ of the vertices: for every arc $uv\in A$, $u<_{\sigma} v$. An \emph{ordered digraph} is a triple $D_{\sigma}=(V,A,\sigma)$ where $D=(V,A)$ is a digraph and $\sigma$ a vertex ordering of $D$. An arc $uv\in A$ in $D_{\sigma}$ is \emph{backward} if $v<_{\sigma} u$, \emph{forward} otherwise. Let $uv$ be a backward arc, then $span(uv)=\{w\in V\mid v<_{\sigma} w<_{\sigma} u\}$. A \emph{certificate} $c(uv)$ in $D_{\sigma}$ is the vertex set of a directed path from $v$ to $u$ only composed of forward arcs. Observe that every vertex $w\in c(uv)$ distinct from $u$ and $v$ belongs to $span(uv)$ and that $c(uv)$ induces a circuit in which $uv$ is the unique backward arc. We abusively say that an arc  belongs to a certificate if its two vertices do. A subset $B \subseteq A$ of \emph{backward arcs} can be \emph{certified} whenever there exists a collection $\{c(e) : e \in B\}$ of \emph{arc-disjoint} certificates.
/tmp/tmp.UyrYkPT7CO/src/section2-tournaments.tex:8:The following Lemma on matching and vertex cover in bipartite graphs will be used in three of our kernels.
