/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:154:\subsection{Kernel size}
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:156:We can now prove that the simple sunflower reduction rule leads to a linear kernel.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:158:\begin{theorem} \label{thm:bit}
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:159:The \BIT{} problem admits a kernel with $5k$ vertices.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:160:%Let $R = (V, \mathcal{B})$ be an instance of \BIT{} such that $|V|> 5k$. There exists an algorithm that either computes an edition of size at most $k$ or answers \textsc{No} in polynomial time. 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:164:We prove that if $|V|> 5k$, then in polynomial time we can either compute an edition set $\mathcal{S}$ of size at most $k$ such that $R\odot\mathcal{S}$ is consistent, or decide that such a set does not exist. To that aim, we first compute a conflict packing  $\mathcal{C}$ of $R$ and then an ordering $\sigma$ satisfying the conditions of Lemma~\ref{lem:realcpbit}. By Lemma~\ref{lem:cpbit}, if $\mathbf{BTI}(R)\leqslant k$, then $|V(\mathcal{C})| \leqslant 4k$. Hence $V'= V \setminus V(\mathcal{C})$ contains at least $k + 1$ vertices that do not belong to any triplet inconsistent with $\sigma$.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:168:%There exists a simple sunflower $\mathcal{S} = \{C_1, \dots, C_m\}$, $m > k$ that can be computed in polynomial time.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:171:Let $t$ be any betweenness constraint inconsistent with $\sigma$ and $V'' =\{d_1, \dots, d_{k+1}\}$ be a subset of $k + 1$ vertices of $V'$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:conflictbit}, for every $d_i\in V"$, $C_{d_i} = V(t) \cup \{d_i\}$ is a conflict. 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:172:By construction of $\sigma$ (see Lemma~\ref{lem:realcpbit}), $t$ is the unique betweenness constraint of $C_{d_i}$ inconsistent with $\sigma$. Hence $\mathcal{S} = \{C_1, \dots, C_{k+1}\}$ is a simple sunflower of center $t$.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:175:Using Rule~\ref{rule:simplesun} we have to edit the center $t \in \mathcal{B}$ of $\mathcal{S}$ according to $\sigma$. Observe that $R\odot_{\sigma}\{t\}$ still contains at least $k + 1$ vertices that do not belong to any betweenness constraint inconsistent with $\sigma$. It follows that the whole set of betweenness constraints inconsistent with $\sigma$ must be edited. Hence if $R$ contains less than $k$  betweenness constraints inconsistent with $\sigma$, then we return a small trivial \textsc{Yes}-instance; otherwise we return a small trivial \textsc{No}-instance.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:180:%Let $t = abc \in \mathcal{B}$ be a triplet and $\sigma$ be an ordering on $V$. Then $t$ is \emph{consistent} with $\sigma$ if $a <_\sigma b <_\sigma c$ or $c <_\sigma b <_\sigma a$. An instance $R = (V,\mathcal{B})$ is \emph{consistent} if there exists an ordering $\sigma$ on $V$ such that every $t \in \mathcal{B}$ is consistent with $\sigma$. If such an ordering does not exist, then $R$ is \emph{inconsistent}. 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:182:%A conflict $C$ is a subset of $V$ such that $R[C]$ is inconsistent. Given an instance $R = (V, \mathcal{B})$ of \BIT{}, editing a triplet $t \in \mathcal{B}$ means modifying its chosen vertex. A set $\mathcal{F}$ of triplets of $\mathcal{B}$ is an \emph{editing set} for $R$ if editing every triplet $t \in \mathcal{B}$ leads to a consistent instance. 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:183:%We use $R_\sigma = (V, \mathcal{B}, \sigma)$ to denote an instance of \BIT{} fixed under some ordering $\sigma$. Given $V'\subseteq V$, we define the \emph{induced instance} $R_\sigma[V']$ as the instance $R[V']$ ordered under $\sigma$. We first prove that such a problem can be characterized in terms of \emph{conflicts} as well. %For a subset $S \subseteq V$, we define $\mathcal{B}[S] = \{t \in \mathcal{B} \mid V(t) \subseteq S\}$ and $B_\sigma[S] = (S, \mathcal{B}[S], \sigma_{|S})$ (\ie the ordering $\sigma$ restricted to elements of $S$). Finally, given an ordered instance $B_\sigma = (V,  \mathcal{B}, \sigma)$ and a triplet $t = \{a,b,c\}$ such that $a \< b \< c$, \emph{editing} $t$ w.r.t. $\sigma$ means that the choice of $t$ is set to $b$. When dealing with an ordered instance the inconsistency of a triplet is always considered w.r.t. $\sigma$.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:192:%($\Leftarrow$) The proof is by induction on the number of vertices. Observe that the statement clearly holds for $n = 4$. Assume that the statement holds for $n - 1$ vertices and let $R = (V, \mathcal{B})$ be an instance of \BIT{} on $n$ vertices that does not contain any conflict on $4$ vertices. Let $d \in V$ be any vertex; by induction hypothesis, we know that $R_d = R[V \setminus \{d\}]$ admits a (unique) consistent ordering $\sigma$. We will prove how to \emph{insert} $d$ in $\sigma$ to obtain a consistent ordering $\sigma_d$ for $B$. \\
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:194:%	Let $a, b, c$ be respectively the first, second and last vertex of $\sigma$. Since $R_d$ is consistent, $abc \in \mathcal{B}$. Moreover, since there are no conflicts on $4$ vertices there is a unique way to insert $d$ in $\sigma$ to obtain an ordering $\sigma_d$ consistent with $R[\{a,b,c,d\}]$. There are several cases to consider: 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:204:%	In the latter case we repeat the previous steps on $\sigma_{|V \setminus \{a\}}$ (\ie the ordering $\sigma$ restraint to $V \setminus \{a\}$). \\
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:206:%	Let $a'b'c'$ be the last triplet considered in the process and assume that $d$ is between $a'$ and $b'$ in $\sigma_d$ (the other cases are similar). We will prove that $\sigma_d$ is a consistent ordering of $B$. Let $t$ be a triplet containing $d$ (observe that the other triplets are consistent with $\sigma_d$ by construction). Assume first that $V(t) = \{a',d,e\}$ with $e \notin \{a',b',c'\}$. Observe that the triplets $\{a',b',d\}$ and $\{a',b',e\}$ are consistent with $\sigma_d$ by construction. This means that $a'db', \{ea'b' \vee a'b'e\} \in \mathcal{B}$: it follows that $t$ is consistent with $\sigma_d$, since otherwise $\{a',b',d,e\}$ would be a conflict on four vertices. Now, assume that $V(t) = \{d,e,f\}$ with $\{e,f\} \notin \{a',b',c'\}$. By the previous arguments we know that the triplets $\{a',d,e\},\ \{a',d,f\}$ and $\{a',e,f\}$ are consistent with $\sigma_d$. It follows that $t$ is consistent with $\sigma_d$ since otherwise $\{a',d,e,f\}$ would be a conflict on four vertices.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:207:%	Since every other case is similar, we have shown that $\sigma_d$ does not contain any inconsistent triplet, implying that $R$ is consistent.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:212:%The main difference with the previous kernelization algorithms relying on the Conflict Packing technique lies in the topology of conflicts for an ordered instance $R_\sigma = (V, \mathcal{B}, \sigma)$. Given an inconsistent triplet $t$ and a vertex $d$ that does not belong to any inconsistent triplet, we do not need to require that $d$ belongs to $span(t)$ to obtain a conflict (recall that $span(t)$ denotes the set of vertices that are contained between $V(t)$ in $\sigma$). As indicated by Lemma~\ref{lem:conflictbit}, \emph{any} such vertex can be used to form a conflict with $V(t)$. 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:216:%\blue{Let $R_{\sigma} = (V, \mathcal{B},\sigma)$ be an ordered dense set of betweenness triplets . If $R_\sigma[\{a,b,c,d\}]$, with $\{a,b,c,d\}\subseteq V$, contains a unique triplet $t$ inconsistent with $\sigma$}, then $\{a,b,c,d\}$ is a conflict.
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:220:%Assume w.l.o.g. that $V(t) = \{a,b,c\}$ and $t = bca\in \mathcal{B}$. It follows that either $a <_\sigma b <_\sigma c$ or  $b <_\sigma a <_\sigma c$ (the cases of reverse orderings are symmetric). Let us denote by $t$ and $t"$ the betweenness triplets such that $V(t')=\{b,c,d\}$ and $V(T")=\{a,c,d\}$. Let us show that the three possibilities for $t'$ leads to a conflict on $\{a,b,c,d\}$:
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:223:%\item $t'=bcd$: If $a <_\sigma b <_\sigma c$, then $t'$ being consistent with $\sigma$ implies that $a <_\sigma b <_\sigma c<_{\sigma} d$. As $t$ is the only betweenness triplet of $R_\sigma[\{a,b,c,d\}]$ inconsistent with $\sigma$, we have that $t"=acd\in\mathcal{B}$. Likewise, if $b <_\sigma a <_\sigma c$ holds, then we have $b <_\sigma a <_\sigma c<_{\sigma} d$ and $t"=acd\in\mathcal{B}$. 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:225:%\item $t'=cbd$: If $a <_\sigma b <_\sigma c$, then $t'$ being consistent with $\sigma$ implies that $d <_\sigma b <_\sigma c$. As $t$ is the only betweenness triplet of $R_\sigma[\{a,b,c,d\}]$ inconsistent with $\sigma$, either $t"=dac\in\mathcal{B}$ or $t"=adc\in\mathcal{B}$. Likewise, if $b <_\sigma a <_\sigma c$ holds, then $t"=dac\in\mathcal{B}$. 
/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:227:%\item $t'=cdb$: If $a <_\sigma b <_\sigma c$, then $t'$ being consistent with $\sigma$ implies that $a<_{\sigma} b<_{\sigma} d<_{\sigma} c$. As $t$ is the only betweenness triplet of $R_\sigma[\{a,b,c,d\}]$ inconsistent with $\sigma$, we have $t"=adc\in\mathcal{B}$. Likewise, if $b <_\sigma a <_\sigma c$ holds, then $t"=dac\in\mathcal{B}$ or $t"=adc\in\mathcal{B}$.  
