/tmp/arxiv_1101.4491_src/section4-betweenness.tex:277:%\paragraph{Conflict Packing.} Using Conflict Packing and the Simple sunflower rule, we prove that the \BIT{} problem can be solved in polynomial time on instances whose parameter satisfies $k < (|V|/5)$. The existence of this algorithm directly implies the existence of a linear vertex-kernel for \BIT{}, as stated in Corollary~\ref{coro:bit}. The notion of Conflict Packing for the \BIT{} problem is similar to the one for \RTI{} (Section~\ref{sec:safe}). Indeed, here again, two conflicts may share a triplet $t$ but still require two distinct editions. Hence, the notion of \emph{seed} is useful in this case too. 
